  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nDry air is working its way in from the west, quickly eroding\nmost of the clouds that brought us rain earlier today. Some low\nclouds north of the Canadian border may make their way into the\nArrowhead for Sunday morning. Otherwise, look for mostly sunny\nskies out there today with highs in the 50s to a few degrees\nover 60. Post-cold frontal gusty winds are expected today too,\nwith the strongest gusts expected in the Arrowhead where the\npressure gradient will be the tightest. \n\nTonight, high pressure settles in and it will bring mostly clear\nskies and light winds. It should be a pretty good radiational\ncooling night. Of course, it's another night where expected lows\nare hovering around freezing, so delineating who gets a Frost\nAdvisory versus who gets a Freeze Warning is the challenge. Much\nof northeast Minnesota away from Lake Superior and north of US-2\nhas the best chance for seeing some around or just below\nfreezing temperatures (~30-80% chance with the highest chances\nin the typical low spots). I was generous with Freeze Warnings\nthere. In Northwest Wisconsin, the best chances for a freeze are\nin interior Iron/Ashland counties and a bit into northern Price\ncounty (30-70% chance), maybe sneaking into parts of\nDouglas/Bayfield/Sawyer/Burnett counties a bit (30-50% chance).\nI think there is some potential for very slightly colder \ntemperatures than most models suggest, but on the other hand, \nif winds remain even a little higher than currently forecast \n(which they could), that could negate cooling a bit. All said, I\nissued a Freeze Warning for Ashland/Iron counties, but left all\nothers as a Frost Advisory. There could be some minor headline \nchanges today depending on how things look with the next \nforecast update early this afternoon.\n\nTemperatures should warm up nicely Monday morning with southerly\nflow developing through the day with warm advection. For now,\nthe forecast is dry, but we'll have to keep an eye on the\npotential for some showers to develop Monday afternoon and\nevening. There may be a little instability associated with the\nwarm air advection that could promote a little rain. Otherwise,\na low pressure system is looking to pass across/just north of\nthe Canadian Border going into Tuesday, and most of the rain\nassociated with that should be well into Ontario. We should see\ntemperatures warm up into the upper 60s and 70s with a warmer\nair mass moving in.\n\nA weak cold front Wednesday should drop temperatures just a\nsmidge, but they'll still be pretty warm for this time of year.\nThere could be some stray wraparound showers, but confidence\nisn't really high at this time. Expect northwest flow Thursday\nwith slightly cooler temperatures, then another warmup with\nsoutherly flow again going into the weekend and possibly a\nlittle rain.",
